China admits it detained a top  international police official after he  mysteriously vanished during a trip home  mung Heung whay resigned as a head of  Interpol that's the global police agency  Beijing says he's being held for its  suspected bribery his detention is now  shining a harsh light on China's  sweeping an authoritarian crackdown on  alleged graft and disloyalty Ben Tracy  is following this story from Beijing Ben  what can you tell us good morning good  morning this was a bold and brazen move  by the Chinese government and it could  have significant consequences some are  now questioning whether or not Chinese  officials should be allowed to leave  international organizations if the  Chinese government can simply make them  vanish now Mong Heung whay was named  president of Interpol in 2016 and was  living in France he came back to China  for a visit last month and that is when  he went missing his wife says before he  disappeared she received this text  message from him showing a knife to warn  her that he was in danger the Chinese  government now admits they detained  Hmong and are accusing him of taking  bribes and other crimes  now Interpol says it was not informed  that Hmong was under investigation  the Chinese only admitted to having him  in custody after Interpol demanded an  explanation Chinese President Xi  Jingping has led a high-profile  crackdown on corruption in the Communist  Party in the past six years 1.5 million  party officials have been punished but  this is also widely viewed as a way for  Xi to take down political rivals and  those he finds disloyal now here in  China the courts answer to the Communist  Party so mungs detention is basically  the same thing as a conviction and he  could even face the death penalty  bianna such an incredible mystery Ben  Tracy and Beijing thank you
 

